WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.203
In this lesson, you'll build your first agent chat UI

2
00:00:03.269 --> 00:00:05.338
on a production grade foundation that you

3
00:00:05.338 --> 00:00:07.374
can customize and continue to expand on.

4
00:00:07.674 --> 00:00:09.976
You'll use CopilotKit, an agent UI,

5
00:00:10.076 --> 00:00:13.580
to connect your React front end to a LangChain Deep Agent

6
00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:15.782
as well as to a Google ADK agent.

7
00:00:15.982 --> 00:00:17.517
Throughout the rest of this course,

8
00:00:17.517 --> 00:00:19.285
we will expand on the foundation here

9
00:00:19.686 --> 00:00:21.688
with a full generative UI spectrum.

10
00:00:21.788 --> 00:00:23.790
All right, let's get coding.

11
00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:25.525
Throughout this course, we're going to augment

12
00:00:25.525 --> 00:00:27.193
our agent beyond the text-only chat experience.

13
00:00:27.293 --> 00:00:28.928
But before we do that, I want to take

14
00:00:28.928 --> 00:00:30.797
a minute to talk about the lasting value of chat.

15
00:00:30.864 --> 00:00:32.932
LLMs first broke through the public imagination

16
00:00:32.932 --> 00:00:34.968
with the launch of ChatGPT in 2022.

17
00:00:35.268 --> 00:00:38.571
And ChatGPT was, of course, first and foremost, a chat experience.

18
00:00:38.972 --> 00:00:42.175
Many people predicted that chat would be a quickly passing fad

19
00:00:42.275 --> 00:00:45.078
and that it would be replaced by a more traditional interaction modality.

20
00:00:45.178 --> 00:00:48.181
But LLMs have come a very long way since the original ChatGPT.

21
00:00:48.581 --> 00:00:51.184
And we're now fully in the age of AI agents.

22
00:00:51.584 --> 00:00:53.987
And yet the chat modality is still here with us.

23
00:00:54.387 --> 00:00:56.089
With far more staying power than many

24
00:00:56.089 --> 00:00:57.791
of us initially gave it credit for.

25
00:00:58.291 --> 00:01:01.494
Why is that? At CopilotKit, we've come to think

26
00:01:01.494 --> 00:01:03.496
of chat as essentially Slack with an agent.

27
00:01:03.596 --> 00:01:07.500
Chat is an open-ended communication medium between intelligent entities,

28
00:01:07.801 --> 00:01:10.804
which doesn't limit you to some predefined ideas with buttons and menus.

29
00:01:11.004 --> 00:01:12.906
It's also how people communicate.

30
00:01:13.006 --> 00:01:15.475
It's how we communicate with each other and sometimes even with ourselves.

31
00:01:15.775 --> 00:01:17.777
And so, all signs are that chat is here to stay

32
00:01:17.777 --> 00:01:19.879
with us for the long run of the agentic revolution.

33
00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.282
But chat doesn't have to be text only.

34
00:01:22.582 --> 00:01:25.785
It can be augmented by Generative UI and in-app interactions,

35
00:01:25.985 --> 00:01:27.921
and it could even be anchored in

36
00:01:27.921 --> 00:01:29.856
voice or video rather than in text.

37
00:01:29.956 --> 00:01:31.357
The ideas we're exploring in this

38
00:01:31.357 --> 00:01:33.026
course are likely to form our foundation

39
00:01:33.026 --> 00:01:35.662
that will continue to hold as the chat medium evolves.

40
00:01:36.129 --> 00:01:38.331
You've likely built a basic chat UI before.

41
00:01:38.665 --> 00:01:41.401
Even if only in a streamlit app while debugging your agent.

42
00:01:41.734 --> 00:01:43.103
The chat app you will build in

43
00:01:43.103 --> 00:01:44.671
this module is different in two major ways.

44
00:01:45.105 --> 00:01:48.675
First, it is built a top a robust, production grade foundation

45
00:01:48.675 --> 00:01:50.777
that you will be able to continue to expand

46
00:01:50.777 --> 00:01:52.879
on to build full stack agents and generative UIs.

47
00:01:53.379 --> 00:01:56.749
Second, you'll be able to use the chat setup you'll create here

48
00:01:56.749 --> 00:01:58.751
with practically any agentic backend

49
00:01:58.751 --> 00:02:00.854
solution across any programming language,

50
00:02:00.954 --> 00:02:03.623
any LLM provider, and any agent harness.

51
00:02:03.823 --> 00:02:05.492
As the ecosystem evolves, you will also

52
00:02:05.492 --> 00:02:07.127
be able to bring the same setup

53
00:02:07.227 --> 00:02:10.630
to any front-end environment. We will continue to expand on the foundation

54
00:02:10.730 --> 00:02:13.933
we will build in this module throughout the remainder of this course.

55
00:02:14.234 --> 00:02:16.136
This course will assume that you already know

56
00:02:16.236 --> 00:02:19.038
how to build an AI agent. We will provide a pre-built agent,

57
00:02:19.139 --> 00:02:21.141
but DeepLearning has lots of great resources

58
00:02:21.241 --> 00:02:23.042
if you want to learn how to build your own.

59
00:02:23.243 --> 00:02:25.745
In this lesson, we will build a LangChain Deep Agent

60
00:02:25.845 --> 00:02:28.348
that we will deploy behind an AG-UI FastAPI

61
00:02:28.615 --> 00:02:30.617
endpoint built in Python.

62
00:02:30.817 --> 00:02:32.619
We will build a React frontend

63
00:02:32.919 --> 00:02:35.922
using CopilotChat component, which ships with CopilotKit,

64
00:02:35.922 --> 00:02:37.757
and which provides a fully featured chat

65
00:02:37.757 --> 00:02:39.626
component that works out of the box.

66
00:02:39.926 --> 00:02:43.429
Lastly, we'll connect our front end to the agent via the CopilotRuntime,

67
00:02:43.429 --> 00:02:45.598
which in this setup, we will host on a Hono server,

68
00:02:45.899 --> 00:02:48.101
which is a lightweight TypeScript server.

69
00:02:48.201 --> 00:02:50.003
In production, you can host the

70
00:02:50.003 --> 00:02:52.105
CopilotRuntime in essentially every standard server setup.

71
00:02:52.105 --> 00:02:54.507
The CopilotRuntime setup is technically optional,

72
00:02:54.507 --> 00:02:56.910
but it is highly recommended for production deployments

73
00:02:56.910 --> 00:02:58.478
due to a combination of security,

74
00:02:58.478 --> 00:03:00.580
performance and scalability requirements.

75
00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:02.148
Every piece of technology you use

76
00:03:02.148 --> 00:03:04.317
in this setup is fully open source.

77
00:03:04.317 --> 00:03:06.386
After we get our LangChain Deep Agent running,

78
00:03:06.386 --> 00:03:08.955
we'll add an additional Google ADK agent.

79
00:03:09.355 --> 00:03:11.157
and connect it to our front end.

80
00:03:11.157 --> 00:03:12.859
So you can learn firsthand how to

81
00:03:12.859 --> 00:03:14.861
swap and add agents into your own applications.

82
00:03:14.861 --> 00:03:17.030
This flexibility is made possible by

83
00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:19.532
the AG-UI protocol. So, what is AG-UI?

84
00:03:19.532 --> 00:03:23.636
AG-UI stands for the Agent-User Interaction Protocol.

85
00:03:23.803 --> 00:03:27.073
It's an open, lightweight, event-based protocol

86
00:03:27.073 --> 00:03:31.010
for facilitating interactions between agentic backends and agentic frontends.

87
00:03:31.211 --> 00:03:33.479
Under the hood, AG-UI compatible agents

88
00:03:33.479 --> 00:03:36.115
emit AG-UI output events as they run,

89
00:03:36.416 --> 00:03:38.618
and they accept AG-UI input events

90
00:03:38.618 --> 00:03:41.821
emitted by frontends in response to relevant user interactions.

91
00:03:42.322 --> 00:03:43.823
The AG-UI protocol was born from

92
00:03:43.823 --> 00:03:45.592
a partnership with LangChain and CrewAI.

93
00:03:45.792 --> 00:03:48.795
This means that you can use these agents side by side

94
00:03:48.795 --> 00:03:50.697
in any user-facing application.

95
00:03:50.997 --> 00:03:53.099
Let's jump into some code and see it in action.

96
00:03:53.366 --> 00:03:56.169
We'll begin by setting up our project dependencies.

97
00:03:56.669 --> 00:03:59.172
First, we'll install the Python dependencies

98
00:03:59.272 --> 00:04:01.274
from the requirements.txt file.

99
00:04:01.975 --> 00:04:04.978
Then, we will install the front-end dependencies.

100
00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:11.150
And finally, we will load the API keys

101
00:04:11.284 --> 00:04:13.353
using our helper function.

102
00:04:14.354 --> 00:04:17.657
Next, we're going to start by building the agentic back end,

103
00:04:17.857 --> 00:04:20.860
the agent itself. We're going to start the server.

104
00:04:21.361 --> 00:04:24.330
First, we'll just create a standard FastAPI server.

105
00:04:24.664 --> 00:04:27.834
Then, we'll create a LangGraphAGUIAgent.

106
00:04:28.334 --> 00:04:32.639
This is an AGUI compatible endpoint for your LangGraph agent.

107
00:04:32.905 --> 00:04:37.905
Now remember, LangGraph is the framework underneath all of LangChain's agentic solutions.

108
00:04:38.911 --> 00:04:40.847
So under the hood here, we're actually

109
00:04:40.847 --> 00:04:43.016
going to be using a LangChain Deep Agent,

110
00:04:43.416 --> 00:04:45.818
but it is implemented on top of

111
00:04:45.818 --> 00:04:48.221
LangGraph and therefore we're using the LangGraphAGUIAgent.

112
00:04:48.421 --> 00:04:51.057
Then we're going to bind our LangChain AGUI

113
00:04:51.057 --> 00:04:54.027
agent into the root path of our FastAPI server.

114
00:04:54.427 --> 00:04:58.131
Finally, we're going to start our server on port 8002.

115
00:04:58.564 --> 00:05:01.768
You will notice that each lesson uses a different port

116
00:05:01.968 --> 00:05:03.770
so that they don't override each other.

117
00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:05.972
All right, now we have our basic skeleton

118
00:05:06.172 --> 00:05:08.274
for our agentic backend setup.

119
00:05:08.508 --> 00:05:11.110
We're now going to populate it with an actual agent.

120
00:05:11.411 --> 00:05:13.413
First, we will define that agent.

121
00:05:13.413 --> 00:05:16.015
We're creating a standard LangChain Deep Agent.

122
00:05:16.416 --> 00:05:18.918
Everything about this setup is completely standard

123
00:05:19.018 --> 00:05:21.220
except for one part that I want to call out.

124
00:05:21.387 --> 00:05:25.391
which is we're injecting CopilotKit's middleware into the LangChain agent.

125
00:05:25.391 --> 00:05:28.194
Middleware is a standard concept in the LangChain universe,

126
00:05:28.194 --> 00:05:32.198
and the CopilotKit middleware allows us to connect our LangChain agent

127
00:05:32.198 --> 00:05:33.900
to everything AG-UI.

128
00:05:34.267 --> 00:05:38.137
Finally, we're going to set the graph

129
00:05:38.137 --> 00:05:40.239
of our previous LangChain agent to the

130
00:05:40.239 --> 00:05:43.443
graph of the agent we just created.

131
00:05:44.577 --> 00:05:46.579
And that's it. We're running.

132
00:05:46.879 --> 00:05:48.981
Now that we've set up our agentic backend,

133
00:05:49.015 --> 00:05:52.285
we're going to get started with the agentic frontend with CopilotKit.

134
00:05:52.585 --> 00:05:54.187
We're going to start the frontend,

135
00:05:54.287 --> 00:05:58.191
and we're going to use our standard helper start_frontend function.

136
00:05:58.424 --> 00:05:59.726
By the way, if you're curious,

137
00:05:59.726 --> 00:06:01.260
you can check out the helper file,

138
00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:03.930
but it really just has a lot of standard boilerplate

139
00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:06.399
for the standard operations we're going to take on here.

140
00:06:06.532 --> 00:06:09.635
Now our frontend is up and running in port 3002.

141
00:06:09.936 --> 00:06:13.172
You'll notice the read the logs statement in this output cell.

142
00:06:13.172 --> 00:06:16.442
If you ever run into an issue with your front end,

143
00:06:16.442 --> 00:06:20.113
you can check out this dev-logs.txt file for the full

144
00:06:20.713 --> 00:06:22.982
logging generated by the front end.

145
00:06:22.982 --> 00:06:25.184
You can view the front end app

146
00:06:25.184 --> 00:06:27.186
by clicking on the link you saw before.

147
00:06:27.186 --> 00:06:29.689
But for convenience, we're also going to display the iframe embedded

148
00:06:29.689 --> 00:06:32.692
right within the Jupyter notebook using the display_app helper function.

149
00:06:32.692 --> 00:06:35.695
You'll see here that the initial state of the front end

150
00:06:35.695 --> 00:06:37.463
is an empty app that just has

151
00:06:37.463 --> 00:06:39.198
the text in the middle of it.

152
00:06:39.198 --> 00:06:42.502
There is no copilot, there's no agents, just an empty starting point.

153
00:06:42.502 --> 00:06:45.705
We're going to continue by setting up the CopilotRuntime.

154
00:06:46.072 --> 00:06:48.174
The CopilotRuntime is a backend that sits in

155
00:06:48.174 --> 00:06:50.610
between your front end and your agent

156
00:06:51.010 --> 00:06:54.881
for security, performance, and engineering scalability reasons.

157
00:06:55.181 --> 00:06:57.884
It is technically not absolutely required.

158
00:06:58.184 --> 00:07:00.319
You could connect your front end directly to your agent,

159
00:07:00.620 --> 00:07:04.891
but for production environments, we highly, highly recommend using the CopilotRuntime.

160
00:07:05.057 --> 00:07:06.559
The first thing we're going to do

161
00:07:06.626 --> 00:07:08.728
is import the LangGraph Http Connector

162
00:07:08.761 --> 00:07:11.330
from the @ag-ui package.

163
00:07:12.131 --> 00:07:14.434
We're then going to use this connector

164
00:07:14.434 --> 00:07:16.536
to point at the LangChain endpoint

165
00:07:16.536 --> 00:07:21.007
we set up earlier at port 8002. Now we're going to

166
00:07:21.007 --> 00:07:23.810
set up a new CopilotRuntime instance.

167
00:07:24.310 --> 00:07:27.513
You'll notice that when we set up the CopilotRuntime,

168
00:07:27.513 --> 00:07:30.216
we specify a dictionary mapping between agent IDs

169
00:07:30.216 --> 00:07:32.485
and actual agent endpoints.

170
00:07:32.485 --> 00:07:36.355
We're setting up the langGraphAgent under the default ID in this case.

171
00:07:36.956 --> 00:07:40.059
Your frontend can specify which agent it's talking to.

172
00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:44.664
If no agent is specified, the default agent is used.

173
00:07:44.897 --> 00:07:48.901
Then we're going to create an endpoint using our CopilotRuntime

174
00:07:48.901 --> 00:07:53.005
and set its basePath to /api/copilotkit.

175
00:07:53.005 --> 00:07:57.777
And lastly, we're going to serve this application using a Hono server.

176
00:07:57.777 --> 00:08:01.080
Now we've got a CopilotRuntime running and we've got an agent running.

177
00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:03.282
It's time to bring the front end up to speed.

178
00:08:03.282 --> 00:08:07.186
We'll begin by wrapping our application with the CopilotKit provider.

179
00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:10.022
When we set up the CopilotKit provider,

180
00:08:10.022 --> 00:08:11.524
we point it at the runtime URL we

181
00:08:11.524 --> 00:08:13.025
just set up in the step before.

182
00:08:13.426 --> 00:08:15.428
Remember, /api/copilotkit.

183
00:08:16.128 --> 00:08:19.332
Any part of your application that talks to agents

184
00:08:19.332 --> 00:08:21.834
must be wrapped by a CopilotKit provider.

185
00:08:22.335 --> 00:08:24.604
You could do this on a page-by-page basis,

186
00:08:24.604 --> 00:08:27.607
but it is usually easiest to do this in a single place

187
00:08:27.607 --> 00:08:30.309
in your layout.tsx file.

188
00:08:30.710 --> 00:08:33.312
So you can wrap your entire application once

189
00:08:33.312 --> 00:08:35.014
and then talk to agents anywhere within it.

190
00:08:35.047 --> 00:08:38.651
Next, we're going to set up the CopilotChat component.

191
00:08:39.552 --> 00:08:42.188
CopilotChat is a built-in, fully featured chat

192
00:08:42.188 --> 00:08:44.857
component that ships with the CopilotKit packages.

193
00:08:44.957 --> 00:08:48.261
You can fully customize it using custom CSS or custom subcomponents,

194
00:08:48.361 --> 00:08:50.530
although we will not be covering that in this course.

195
00:08:50.930 --> 00:08:54.333
Or if should you choose to, you could use the useAgent hook

196
00:08:54.634 --> 00:08:58.304
to build a fully featured chat interface from scratch.

197
00:08:58.604 --> 00:09:00.039
Now that we've set up the chat, let's give

198
00:09:00.039 --> 00:09:01.607
it a try. Hello, how can I help you today?

199
00:09:01.807 --> 00:09:03.409
All right, we've just built a chat

200
00:09:03.409 --> 00:09:05.011
that connects to a real LangChain agent.

201
00:09:05.311 --> 00:09:10.311
Now, remember, AGUI lets you connect to any agentic backends out there.

202
00:09:10.750 --> 00:09:12.852
So we're now going to demonstrate this

203
00:09:12.852 --> 00:09:15.254
by connecting the same application

204
00:09:15.254 --> 00:09:16.956
to a Google ADK agent. We're

205
00:09:16.956 --> 00:09:18.958
going to set up an ADK agent.

206
00:09:19.258 --> 00:09:22.962
First, we're going to create a standard Gemini ADK agent

207
00:09:23.262 --> 00:09:24.964
by importing the LlmAgent

208
00:09:24.964 --> 00:09:26.966
from the standard Google ADK packages.

209
00:09:27.266 --> 00:09:29.969
Next, we're going to set up an AGUI

210
00:09:30.670 --> 00:09:34.240
server for this raw agent.

211
00:09:34.607 --> 00:09:39.512
We're using the ADKAgent class imported from the ag_ui_adk package.

212
00:09:40.079 --> 00:09:43.182
Next, we're going to set up a plain old FastAPI server

213
00:09:43.449 --> 00:09:48.449
and add our AGUI endpoint for the gemini_agent to its root path.

214
00:09:49.221 --> 00:09:52.158
Finally, we're going to start the server on port 8009.

215
00:09:52.491 --> 00:09:54.160
All right, so now we have

216
00:09:54.160 --> 00:09:57.063
our old LangChain agent running in the background.

217
00:09:57.463 --> 00:10:00.032
And we have another agent that is being

218
00:10:00.032 --> 00:10:02.969
served with its own FastAPI server on port 8009.

219
00:10:03.069 --> 00:10:06.238
We're now going to update the CopilotRuntime to register this new agent.

220
00:10:06.639 --> 00:10:09.342
Because we're using TypeScript inside of this Jupyter notebook,

221
00:10:09.642 --> 00:10:11.410
we cannot update the code from

222
00:10:11.410 --> 00:10:13.446
before, we must overwrite the file altogether.

223
00:10:13.779 --> 00:10:15.481
The majority of what you see here

224
00:10:15.481 --> 00:10:17.183
is identical to what you saw before.

225
00:10:17.583 --> 00:10:20.286
Let me highlight the two places where we've changed the code.

226
00:10:20.386 --> 00:10:22.655
First, we're going to set up a connector

227
00:10:22.655 --> 00:10:24.957
to the agent endpoint we just set up,

228
00:10:25.458 --> 00:10:27.627
running at port 8009. And then we're going

229
00:10:27.627 --> 00:10:29.762
to add it to the dictionary of agents

230
00:10:29.862 --> 00:10:33.566
served by the CopilotRuntime, this time under the Gemini ID.

231
00:10:33.866 --> 00:10:35.968
There's nothing special about the Gemini ID.

232
00:10:36.268 --> 00:10:38.104
You could use any string you want.

233
00:10:38.104 --> 00:10:39.905
Now we're going to update the frontend

234
00:10:40.006 --> 00:10:43.609
to use the new Gemini agent we've set up.

235
00:10:44.010 --> 00:10:47.713
Once again, because we're editing TypeScript files, we have to override them.

236
00:10:48.014 --> 00:10:50.116
You'll see here, the only change we're

237
00:10:50.116 --> 00:10:52.218
making is that we've changed the agentId

238
00:10:52.218 --> 00:10:54.620
to point at the Gemini identifier.

239
00:10:54.987 --> 00:10:57.790
that we set up earlier for our Gemini agent.

240
00:10:58.491 --> 00:11:03.396
That's it. Now, let's display the app one more time

241
00:11:04.463 --> 00:11:07.767
and let's ask it what model is it powered by.

242
00:11:13.606 --> 00:11:16.008
All right, it's a large language model trained by Google.

243
00:11:16.308 --> 00:11:17.610
For those who don't know, Google

244
00:11:17.610 --> 00:11:19.111
is the maker of the Gemini models.

245
00:11:19.412 --> 00:11:21.714
So you see here, we took an existing chat UI

246
00:11:21.814 --> 00:11:23.282
and pointed it at a different

247
00:11:23.282 --> 00:11:25.017
agent with almost no work at all.

248
00:11:25.117 --> 00:11:27.086
That's it. On to the next one.
